==English==
{{wp|<dab>}}

===Etymology===
{{inh+|en|enm|savete}}, from {{der|en|fro|sauveté}}, from earlier {{m|fro|salvetet}}, from {{der|en|la-med|salvitās}}, {{m|la|salvitātem}}, from {{der|en|la|salvus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈseɪfti/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-safety.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪfti|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The condition or feeling of being [[safe]]; [[security]]; [[certainty]].
#: {{ant|en|danger}}
#: {{ux|en|If you [[push it]] [[to the limit]], '''safety''' is not guaranteed.}}
#: {{ux|en|He managed to reach the border and escape to '''safety'''.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|date=May 15 2016|actor=w:John Oliver|title=w:Last Week Tonight with John Oliver|season=3|number=12|episode=911|network=HBO|text=Oh, oh! “Go to '''safety'''”! Why didn’t I think of that⁉ Here I am in danger when, really, I could simply be going to '''safety'''! I shouldn’t have wasted your time by calling in the first place!}}
# {{lb|en|mechanics}} A [[safety lock]] or [[safety catch]]: a [[mechanism]] on a weapon or dangerous [[equipment]] designed to prevent [[accidental]] firing or operation.
#: {{ux|en|Be sure that the '''safety''' is set before proceeding.}}
# Preservation from escape; close custody.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King John|IV|2|text={{...}} imprison him, {{...}} / Deliver him to '''safety'''; and return,}}
# {{lb|en|sports}}
## {{lb|en|American football}} An instance of a player being [[sacked]] or [[tackled]] in the end zone, or stepping out of the end zone and off the field, resulting in two points to the opposite [[team]].
##: {{ux|en|He sacked the quarterback in the end zone for a '''safety'''.}}
## {{lb|en|American football}} Any of the defensive [[players]] who are in [[position]] furthest from the line of [[scrimmage]] and whose responsibility is to [[defend]] [[against]] passes as well as to be the tacklers of last resort.
##: {{ux|en|The free '''safety''' made a game-saving tackle on the runner who had broken past the linebackers.}}
## {{lb|en|baseball}} A [[safety squeeze]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1952|author=w:Bernard Malamud|title=w:The Natural|publisher=Time Life Books|year_published=1966|page=225|url=https://archive.org/details/thenatural00mala
|passage=Boy wondered about that bunt. He had a notion Fowler would commit himself soon because time was on the go. But Fowler didn’t, making it another sweep of three Pirates. He had thus far given up only two '''safeties'''.}}
## {{lb|en|snooker}} A [[safety shot]] or sequence of such shots.
##: {{ux|en|That was a poor '''safety''' from Higgins.}}
##: {{ux|en|After thirty minutes of '''safety''', Selby finally potted a red, to ironic cheers.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} A [[safety bicycle]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1897|journal=American Architect and Architecture|volume=57-58|page=51
|passage=Many wheelmen and wheelwomen, riding '''safeties''', tandems and tricycles, stopped there during the evening and we had good opportunity for comparing American and English bicycles {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=[[s:The Cycle Industry|The Cycle Industry]]|author=W. F. Grew|year=1921|location=London|page=22
|text=There are cyclists, like Mr. F. T. Bidlake, who prefer a tricycle to a '''safety''', and to such men a long ride on a tricycle is no more fatiguing than a safety bicycle ride of the same distance.}}

